<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Transcribed by Divyash Maheshwari -->
<worksheet xml:id="se1j">
  <title>Linear Algebra Sample Exam 1J</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Work done on scratch paper will not be graded. You don't need to show your work on pages 1 and 2.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>

<page>
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (5 points) Indicate whether the situations are <term>possible</term> or <term>impossible</term>.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col width="76%" />
        <col width="12%" />
        <col width="12%" />
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell>possible</cell>
            <cell>impossible</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>a) <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent. The columns of <m>A</m> span a line, and the columns of <m>B</m> span a different line.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>b) Vectors <m>\vec{x}</m> and <m>\vec{y}</m> are linearly independent and parallel.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>c) Matrix <m>A</m> is <m>m \times n</m>, <m>A\mathbf{x} = A\mathbf{y}</m>, <m>\mathbf{x} \text{ and } \mathbf{y}</m> are vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, <m>\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y}</m>, and every column of <m>A</m> is pivotal.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>d) <m>T(\vec{x})</m> is a one-to-one matrix transform, <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, <m>T(\vec{x}) = T(\vec{y})</m>, and <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{y}</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>e) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform, <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 3</m> with a pivot in every column, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a non-trivial solution.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0.1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (7 points) Indicate <term>true</term> if the statement is true, otherwise, indicate <term>false</term>.
      </p>
      <tabular>
      <col width="88%"/>
      <col width="6%"/>
      <col width="6%"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell></cell>
          <cell>true</cell>
          <cell>false</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>a) The transform <m>T(x_1, x_2) = (x_1 + x_2, x_1 - x_2)</m> is a linear transform.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>b) If <m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, <m>\vec{x}</m> and <m>\vec{y}</m> are vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{y}</m>, and <m>A\vec{x} = A\vec{y}</m>, then <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> cannot be one-to-one.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>c) If the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has infinitely many solutions for a given vector <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m>, then the homogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> also has infinitely many solutions.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>d) The span of two linearly independent vectors <m>\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> is equal to the span of <m>\{\vec{v}_1 + \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_1 - \vec{v}_2\}</m>.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>e) If the linear system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is consistent, <m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, and the columns of <m>A</m> are <m>\vec{a}_i</m>, then there must be scalars <m>c_i</m> for <m>i = 1, 2, 3, \ldots, n</m> so that <m>\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^n c_i\vec{a}_i = \vec{b}</m>.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>f) If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has a unique solution for a specific vector <m>\vec{b}</m>, then <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>g) If two matrices have the same pivot columns, then the two matrices are row equivalent.</p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>

<page>
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} -2 &amp; 4 \\ 1 &amp; -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>. In the grids below, sketch a) the span of the columns of <m>A</m> and b) the solution set of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>. You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="4% 32% 4% 32%">  
          <p> (a) </p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1j-3a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p> (b)</p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1j-3b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          </sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>(7 points) Fill in the blanks. You do not need to justify your reasoning.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> and <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform that first rotates points in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> clockwise about the origin by <m>3\pi / 2</m> radians and then reflects them through the line <m>x_1 = 0</m>, then <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 &amp; a_2 \\ a_3 &amp; a_4 \end{pmatrix}</m> where <m>a_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>a_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>a_3 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m> and <m>a_4 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Matrix <m>A</m> is non-zero, <m>2 \times 2</m>, in RREF, has only <m>1</m> pivot column, and every entry of <m>A</m> is either <m>1</m> or <m>0</m>. How many different matrices can you make that meet all of these criteria? <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is onto, where <m>T_A \colon \mathbb{R}^6 \to \mathbb{R}^3</m>, then <m>A</m> has exactly <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m> pivots, the domain of <m>T_A</m> is <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, the co-domain of <m>T_A</m> is <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, and the range of <m>T_A</m> is <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 8</m> matrix, <m>b \in \mathbb{R}^3</m> is in the span of the columns of <m>A</m>, and <m>x \mapsto Ax \in \mathbb{R}^3</m> is onto, then how many free variables does <m>Ax = b</m> have? <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\vec{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\vec{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, and <m>\vec{y} = \begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ y_3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. If <m>A</m> has <m>2</m> pivots, the transform <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> satisfies <m>T(\vec{v}_1) = 2T(\vec{v}_2) \neq \vec{0}</m>, <m>y_3 = 5</m>, and <m>\vec{y}</m> satisfies <m>A\vec{y} = \vec{0}</m>, then <m>y_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>y_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; h &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\vec{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ k \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\vec{x} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}</m>. Then <m>\left( \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 &amp; h &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; k \end{array} \right)</m> is a representation of the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. The solution set of this system is the <m>y\text{-axis}</m> when <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m> and <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the linear system <m>2x + hy = 5</m> and <m>6x + 12y = k</m>. The system will have no solutions when <m>k = 0</m> and <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>. But the system will have an infinite number of solutions when <m>h = 4</m> and <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</page>

<page>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Suppose <m>\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\}</m> is a linearly independent set of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>. Indicate whether the following sets are linearly independent or linearly dependent. You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
      <tabular>
      <col width="35%" />
      <col width="25"/>
      <col width="25%" />
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell></cell>
          <cell>linearly independent</cell>
          <cell>linearly dependent</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>a) <m>\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>b) <m>\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3, \vec{0}\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>c) <m>\{\vec{v}_1 + \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>d) <m>\{\vec{v}_1 + \vec{v}_2, 2\vec{v}_1 + 2\vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3</m></p></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Please show your work for this question.
      </p>
      <p>
        Determine all values of <m>k</m>, if any, so that the span of the vectors is only a plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. Assume <m>k</m> is a real number. Please show your work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          v_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -k \end{pmatrix} \text{, } v_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} \text{, } v_3 = \begin{pmatrix} k \\ -1 \\ -5k \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>